From 52a000e9a92a8772c2baadda2d055c2b7518b11f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Lovelace's Square <noreply@lovelacesquare.org>
Date: Sun, 18 Oct 2026 14:18:13 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 5/5] mcp: --automation-read-root and --automation-write-root
 confine the files an agent reaches

`deckcraft-cli mcp` let an agent open, insert, save and export any file
the user can. As in WordCraft and GridCraft, the headless server now
takes a read root and a write root, and every path a command reads or
writes must lie inside the matching one.

- engine: file_access (from WordCraft): AutomationRoots. A new io module
  is the one way engine commands reach a named file: io::read_file
  (file.open, and insert.picture/audio/video, picture.change and picture
  fills through media_bytes), io::write_file (file.save, file.saveAs,
  file.export to deckcraft, pptx, outline, PDF and images; the
  write-then-rename save moved here from cmd/file.rs) and io::file_len
  (File > Info). Paths are made absolute and their links and junctions
  followed (for a missing file, its nearest existing folder's), then
  compared by whole components, without regard to case on Windows; `..`
  below a missing folder, dangling links and Windows device names are
  refused. A root left out grants no access of its kind. Roots apply
  per thread (confine) or to the whole process (confine_process). A
  test keeps engine code other than io and recovery (the app's own
  AutoRecover folder) from touching the file system.
- mcp: Headless::with_automation_roots runs every call confined; a
  slide rendered to a `path` is written through io::write_file.
- cli: `mcp --automation-read-root DIR --automation-write-root DIR`
  (`--flag DIR` or `--flag=DIR`, once; folders must exist; a malformed
  flag is an error, never a root silently left out); confines the whole
  process and says so on stderr. `--sample` is opened before. With
  --connect the roots are refused: they don't confine a running app.
- docs: mcp.md, README.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
